faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules keyed on incoming interface eth2 (the `r6_r3` link connecting router6 to router3).

**Mechanism:** Router6's eth2 is the sole path for traffic originating outside the mswitch2 domain to reach the 172.18.51.x subnet housing pc15–pc18 (and the switch11-side laptops). Three iptables rules — `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and a catch-all `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` — silently discard every packet arriving from the router3 side that needs forwarding onward to mswitch2. Outbound packets from pc15–pc18 egress normally through router6 toward router3, but every return packet entering router6 on eth2 hits the FORWARD DROP and is silently discarded. The result is an asymmetric blackhole: pings leave pc15–pc18 and reach their destinations, but no reply ever returns. This explains why `ping -c2` from pc15 to direct IPs timed out rather than completing with "100% packet loss" — the ping process waited indefinitely for replies that were silently dropped inside router6.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, eth2 (`r6_r3` link). All three rules land on the same interface, so removing or replacing them is a single targeted fix. Additionally, router6's RIP configuration only advertises `network 10.0.0.0/8`, leaving the 172.18.51.x subnets unadvertised to the rest of the topology.

**Root cause:** iptables FORWARD DROP rules on router6.eth2 block all return traffic from router3 to the mswitch2-side networks, making pc15–pc18 effectively unreachable for two-way communication.

**Note on the reported symptom:** The user's complaint targets pc11–pc14 and their gateway 192.168.200.1 (router4.eth0). That segment is currently healthy — no tc rules, no iptables, no flap scripts, no resource pressure, stable RIP routes, and 192.168.200.1 responds with 0% loss. No mechanism for the described intermittent hiccup was found on router4.

**Fix:** On router6, flush the three FORWARD DROP rules on eth2: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP; iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP; iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. Also expand the RIP network statement to include the 172.18.x.x range.
=== END ===